  The first question is where the filter controls go, which is easier to judge by looking. I've put three placement
  wireframes in a browser tab, which should open on its own. If it doesn't, here's the link:

  [94m]8;id=1dlcdvx;http://localhost:60358/?key=07cffe201458369039bccdaa932bfe446fa9100a3264ff21d97cdfcbb2733269\http://localhost:60358/?key=07cffe201458369039bccdaa932bfe446fa9100a3264ff21d97cdfcbb2733269[39m]8;;\

  (The browser tab uses a lot of tokens. Tell me if you'd rather keep everything in the terminal.)

  The three options:

  - [1mA. Filter bar between the intro and the table.[0m Everything is always visible: an event type dropdown, a search field
    for device or location, and a "Failed sign-ins only" checkbox. A "Showing N of M events" line is read out to screen
    readers when the results change. This is what I recommend. It makes failed sign-ins easy to find, which is what the
    support tickets were about.
  - [1mB. A single "Filter" button that opens a popover.[0m The page looks the same until someone opens it, and active filters
    show as removable tags. It costs an extra click and needs focus handling for the popover.
  - [1mC. Quick-filter groups plus search.[0m Segmented buttons for All, Sign-ins, Failed sign-ins and Account changes. It's
    the fastest to use, but you can't filter to just one of the nine event types unless we add it to A as well.

  The wireframes only show placement. The real controls will follow [38;5;153mdocs/ui-guidelines/[39m.

  Have a look and tell me what you think here in the terminal. Clicking an option in the browser also records your
  choice.

  Two notes:
